> Taking a cue from the Lord of the Rings, whenever an orc should happen to 
> don or wield an item of elvish make then that orc would be blasted as if 
> the player was playing a knight who happened to pick up Stormbringer. Also 
> should an orc eat a lembas wafer or an elf corpse they would be affected as 
> if by poison, overriding their natural poison resistance.

Isn't that a little unfair to players who choose to play orcs, denying
them both elven equipment _and_ sleep resistance?

What sense does it make for orcs to be poisoned by elves and elf-food,
anyway? Orcs, at least by Nethack's lights, have rather strong
stomachs, do they not? And Elves simply _aren't poisonous_.
The rest doesn't make any sense, either, come to that. Especially when
one considers the vast differences between Nethack and Tolkien; in
particular, the fact that in Nethack Elves are not the natural enemies
of orcs (which really isn't true in the Lord of the Rings, either, as
I recall), but rather simply another race on the same side of the
lawful/chaotic battle. With the two races as allies (more or less),
this all seems a little bit incoherent.

If you simply must make some sort of war-between-the-species, pit
dwarves against your choice of elves or goblinoids (both make a
certain amount of sense and have precedent elsewhere). The bit about
eating corpses is still pretty random, but as far as equipment goes,
it could be reasonably workable- and fair to members of both races.

